a font can be asked which face covers a character

CTFontCreateForString returned the font it was given, always, and
CTFontCopyDefaultCascadeListForLanguages returns an empty array. Both were deliberate and both said
so in their own comments, and together they mean this port has NO font fallback: a character the
chosen face has no glyph for is drawn as nothing at all.

That is visible. The UI family list resolves to TeX Gyre Heros on this system, a Helvetica clone
with Latin and BASIC Greek, so Money Manager EX draws its Arabic and Cyrillic language names as
nothing and drops accented Greek out of the middle of a word while unaccented Greek survives (#216).

O2FontPatternCoveringCharacter asks fontconfig for a face that really has one code point.

  MEMOISED BY CODE POINT, and that is not an optimisation. FcFontMatch measured 28 percent of all
  samples in a LibreOffice text conversion, and this sits on a path that could run per character.
  A code point is the right key because what decides the face is the SCRIPT.

  IT CHECKS THE ANSWER. FcFontMatch never fails, it SUBSTITUTES, so for a script nothing installed
  covers it hands back a face without the character and returning that would swap the face and
  still draw nothing. FcCharSetHasChar on the match is what makes the reply honest.

  The answer is FcNameUnparse of the match, which carries file=, so everything downstream takes the
  fast path in +filenameForPattern: rather than matching a second time.

CTFontCreateForString now uses it for the FIRST character in the range the current font has no
glyph for, which is the contract: the reply only has to cover the beginning of the range. Every
path still ends at the current font, retained, because a caller asking for a fallback is in a loop
and nil invites it to ask again.

NOTHING VISIBLE CHANGES YET, and that is measured rather than hoped: iA Writer, LibreOffice and
Money Manager EX import CTFontCreateForString ZERO times, and nothing in this tree calls it. The
comment this replaces claimed LibreOffice loops on it; its import table does not agree.

So this is the first half. The second half is making the LAYOUT path use it, and that is recorded
in #216 rather than started here, because a glyph index is FACE RELATIVE:
-[O2Context_builtin_FT showGlyphs:advances:count:] rasterises a whole run through one FT_Face, so
per glyph substitution changes a CGContext level contract and does not belong in a drawing loop.

